// EXPECTED TO FAIL under `--library`: the `consistency:` declaration names a theorem that
// does not exist. A library's definitions must be sound, and a declaration pointing at
// nothing settles nothing.
//
// The sibling half of this check — a multi-clause block with NO declaration at all — is not
// enforced yet: the obligations cannot be paid until the synthesizer lands, and turning it on
// early would fail `std --library` on all 72 multi-clause blocks at once. See
// design/definition-wellformedness.md's build order.
sort N
const Z: N
oper s(a: N) => N

// COVERAGE on an abstract sort. `N` has `Z`/`s` declared and nothing saying it is inductively
// generated, so the arms' `Z` / `s(n)` split is not PROVABLE here — the same thing the
// termination work found. Stated as an axiom, which is the honest form: it is the assumption a
// two-arm split over this sort always rested on.
axiom nIsZeroOrSucc: forall n: N; n = Z or (exists m: N; n = s(m))

// NOT RECURSIVE: the step arm returns `s(Z)` rather than recursing. What this fixture tests
// is that a `consistency:` declaration naming a theorem which DOES NOT EXIST is rejected
// under `--library`, and that needs only a multi-clause block with a declaration — the
// recursion was incidental scaffolding. A recursive block now owes a `termination:`
// obligation that an ABSTRACT sort cannot discharge: nothing here says `N` is inductively
// generated, so a predecessor chain need never reach `Z`.
oper f(n: N) => N:
  f(Z) = Z;
  f(s(n)) = s(Z).
  consistency: noSuchTheorem
  totality(peano): fCovers

intheory fCovers
theorem fCovers: forall n: N; n = Z or (exists n_at1: N; n = s(n_at1))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall n: N; n = Z or (exists n_at1: N; n = s(n_at1))
    [by cite nIsZeroOrSucc]
qed
